{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "כי קרן עור פניו", :translit "ki karan or panav", :gloss "for the skin of his face sent forth rays"} {:he "משכותי כפי עליך", :translit "moschoti afi alayich", :gloss "I drew you to Me upon you"} {:he "קרנים מידו לו ושם חביון עוזו", :translit "karnim mihu lo v'sham chivyon uzo", :gloss "rays from Him to him and there the hiding of His strength"} {:he "לוח", :translit "luach", :gloss "tablet"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "קרנים מידו לו ושם חביון עוזו", :spells "לוח"}], :frame {:text "Shabbat 88a-b records that at Sinai an angel forced each Israelite to accept the Torah, while a contrasting tradition in the same passage speaks of Israel accepting it willingly. The phrase 'I drew you to Me' does not appear in Exodus 20 at Sinai; the expression of divine drawing is found in Jeremiah 31:2, 'I have drawn you with lovingkindness.' The midrashic tradition (Shemot Rabbah 47:6) connects the radiance of Moses' face to the light created on the first day, which was hidden away. The Ba'al HaTurim fuses these strands: the tablets themselves are an acronym encoding 'the hiding of His strength,' linking the hidden primordial light to the written tablets Moses now carries.", :sources ["Shabbat 88a-b" "Jeremiah 31:2" "Shemot Rabbah 47:6"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :rashei-tevot, :phrase "קרנים מידו לו ושם חביון עוזו", :spells "לוח", :letters ["ק" "מ" "ל" "ו" "ח" "ע"], :by :selah, :order :mismatch}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:21:00.483369345Z", :model "glm-5.2"}